				<h1 id="versionHeadLine" class="headline">

Elections		</h1>
	
		<div id="ccccc0d002-b1f3-439d-9211-8d24638bbb31" data-containerid="ccc0d002-b1f3-439d-9211-8d24638bbb31" class="pageContent cpGrid cpGrid24"  data-contentcontainerbreakpoint="20" >
			

		<div class="row outer wide"><!--
--><div class="outer col col24 first last" style="" data-widgetcontainerid="cbdb9995-de16-48b5-aa0a-2a4f4376f455">				
	<div class="row nest first last wide">
	
	<div class="inner col col24 first last id4506644b-f7c0-4820-abd8-e64f626d9771" style="" data-widgetcontainerid="4506644b-f7c0-4820-abd8-e64f626d9771">	
<div class="widget editor pageStyles narrow" id="divEditor88501f2a-d29d-4f92-9fbf-227b9fed61e6" data-elementqueryclasses='{"wide-narrow":"min-width:25em"}'>
	    <div class="fr-view">


        <h2 class="subhead1" data-pasted="true">Learn About the Town&apos;s Elections</h2><p>Town elections are held in November of even-numbered years and are coordinated and administered by El Paso County. Coordinated elections may include the election of Town Council members, ballot questions and issues, and referred ordinances or resolutions. The Town Council may also call a special election, if necessary, by ordinance or resolution. Special elections may be held on certain Tuesdays outside the regular election cycle, subject to timing restrictions established by state law. The Town Clerk serves as the Designated Election Official for the Town of Monument.</p><h2 class="subhead1">November 3, 2026 Regular Municipal Election</h2><p>At the upcoming November 3, 2026, Regular Municipal Election, Monument voters will elect five officials: the mayor, two at-large councilmembers, one district 1 councilmember, and one district 2 councilmember. The mayor, district 1 councilmember, and district 2 councilmember will each be elected to four-year terms. For the two at-large councilmember seats, the candidate receiving the highest number of votes will be elected to a four-year term, and the candidate receiving the second-highest number of votes will be elected to a two-year term. Assigning one shorter at-large term preserves the Charter&apos;s staggered term structure and ensures that the two at-large seats do not expire at the same time in future elections.</p><table style="width: 56%; margin: auto; border-width: 1px; border-style: solid;"><colgroup><col style="width: 100%;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><td><strong>Candidate(s) for mayor</strong><br></td></tr><tr><td>Laura Kronick, Kenneth Kimple</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Candidate(s) for district 1 councilmembe​r&nbsp;</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Dan DeBoo</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Candidate(s) for district 2 councilmember</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Marco P. Fiorito</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Candidate(s) for at-large councilmember</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Alex Valderrama, Scott Gilson</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 class="subhead1">Ballot Issue Referred by Town Council</h2><p>In addition to electing Town officials, eligible electors will have the opportunity to vote on a ballot issue referred to voters by the Town Council through Ordinance No. 19-2026. The ballot measure proposes increasing the Town&apos;s Use Tax on construction and building materials and construction equipment from 2.0% to 3.5%, matching the Town&apos;s existing 3.5% sales tax rate on retail purchases.&nbsp;</p><h3 class="subhead2">Official Ballot Title</h3><p>SHALL TOWN OF MONUMENT TAXES BE INCREASED BY $2,272,500 ANNUALLY, BEGINNING JANUARY 1, 2027, AND BY WHATEVER ADDITIONAL AMOUNTS THE TAX RATE &ensp;GENERATES IN EACH YEAR AFTER BY RAISING THE TOWN&apos;S USE TAX ON CONSTRUCTION AND BUILDING MATERIALS AND CONSTRUCTION EQUIPMENT FROM 2.0% TO &nbsp;3.5%, TO MATCH THE TOWN&apos;S EXISTING 3.5% SALES TAX RATE ON RETAIL PURCHASES; AS SET FORTH IN AND SUBJECT TO THE EXEMPTIONS IN ORDINANCE NO. 19-2026 &ensp;AND CHAPTER 3.10 OF THE MUNICIPAL CODE; THE INCREASED USE TAX IS COLLECTED ON CONSTRUCTION AND BUILDING MATERIALS AND CONSTRUCTION EQUIPMENT ONLY WHEN A BUILDING PERMIT OR RIGHT-OF-WAY PERMIT IS REQUESTED AND, WHEN A MUNICIPAL SALES TAX OF 3.5% WAS NOT COLLECTED WHERE THE CONSTRUCTION AND BUILDING MATERIALS OR CONSTRUCTION EQUIPMENT WAS PURCHASED, AND ALL REVENUE WILL BE SPENT ON CAPITAL PROJECTS, INCLUDING:</p><ul><li>STREETS, ROADS, BRIDGES, AND SIDEWALKS; AND</li><li>STORMWATER IMPROVEMENTS;</li></ul><p>AND SHALL THE TOWN BE AUTHORIZED TO COLLECT, RETAIN, AND SPEND THIS TAX REVENUE AND ANY INVESTMENT EARNINGS ON IT AS A VOTER-APPROVED REVENUE CHANGE UNDER ARTICLE X, SECTION 20 OF THE COLORADO CONSTITUTION (TABOR) OR ANY OTHER LAW?</p><h3 class="subhead2"><strong><span style='font-size:19px;font-family:"Open Sans",sans-serif;color:#333366;'>Voter Registration</span></strong></h3><p><span style='font-family:"Open Sans",sans-serif;color:black;'>Information on voter registration and updating your registration is available at&nbsp;</span><span style="color:black;"><a href="http://www.govotecolorado.gov/"><span style='font-family:"Open Sans",sans-serif;color:#8E591C;'>www.GoVoteColorado.gov</span></a></span><span style='font-family:"Open Sans",sans-serif;color:black;'>.&nbsp;</span></p>
    </div>

